00:00:29.953 --> 00:00:35.073
in people's cars, people have them on their wrists, they might be in your backpack right now.

00:00:36.273 --> 00:00:39.793
But what makes a computer a computer? What does make a computer a computer?

00:00:39.937 --> 00:00:41.537
computer anyway.

00:00:41.537 --> 00:00:42.697
And how does it even work?

00:00:49.922 --> 00:00:55.922
I've been working with computers since I was maybe seven years old and now I work on virtual reality.

00:00:59.907 --> 00:01:06.907
[Music]

00:01:06.907 --> 00:01:09.891
As humans, we've always built tools to help us solve...

00:01:09.891 --> 00:01:14.851
problems. Tools like a wheelbarrow, a hammer, or a printing press, or a tractor trailer.

00:01:16.211 --> 00:01:19.331
All of these inventions helped us with manual work.

00:01:19.331 --> 00:01:19.811
Over time,

00:01:19.876 --> 00:01:24.036
people began to wonder if a machine could be designed and built to help us

00:01:24.036 --> 00:01:28.236
with the thinking work we do, like solving equations or tracking the stars

00:01:28.236 --> 00:01:29.860
in the sky.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:34.100
Rather than moving or manipulating physical things like dirt and stone,

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:37.700
these machines would need to be designed to manipulate information.

00:01:39.845 --> 00:01:43.725
As the pioneers of computer science explored how to design a thinking machine,

00:01:43.725 --> 00:01:46.885
they realized that it had to perform four different tasks.

00:01:48.085 --> 00:01:49.765
It would need to take...

00:01:49.829 --> 00:01:56.949
input, store information, process it, and then output the results.

00:01:56.949 --> 00:01:59.789
Now this might sound simple, but these four things.

00:01:59.814 --> 00:02:02.054
Things are common to all computers.

00:02:02.054 --> 00:02:09.794
And that's what makes a computer a computer.

00:02:09.799 --> 00:02:15.459
metal with mechanical levers and gears. By the 20th century though computers

00:02:15.459 --> 00:02:19.783
started using electrical components. These early computers were really large.

00:02:19.783 --> 00:02:24.983
really slow. A computer the size of a room might take hours just to do a basic math problem.

00:02:27.383 --> 00:02:29.703
These machines are things of gleaming very

00:02:29.768 --> 00:02:32.768
very colored metal and numerous flashing lights.

00:02:39.752 --> 00:02:41.312
calculating numbers back then.

00:02:41.312 --> 00:02:47.272
But now we can use them to talk to each other, we can use them to play games, control robots,

00:02:47.272 --> 00:02:49.712
and do any crazy thing that you could probably imagine.

00:02:49.737 --> 00:02:56.737
Modern computers look nothing like those clunky old machines, but they still do these same four things.

00:02:59.721 --> 00:03:02.961
(upbeat music)

00:03:02.961 --> 00:03:04.521
First we're gonna talk about input.

00:03:04.521 --> 00:03:07.321
This is my favorite because what input is,

00:03:07.321 --> 00:03:09.706
is the stuff that the world does or that.

00:03:09.706 --> 00:03:19.691
you do that makes the computer do stuff.

00:03:19.691 --> 00:03:23.651
on your wrist, it might listen to your heartbeat or in your car, it might be

00:03:23.651 --> 00:03:28.331
listening to what the car is doing and a touchscreen can actually sense your

00:03:28.331 --> 00:03:29.675
finger and it takes that

00:03:29.675 --> 00:03:39.660
as input on what it's doing.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:42.260
stored in memory.

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:45.340
A computer's processor takes information from memory.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:48.460
It manipulates it or changes it using an algorithm, which is

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:49.644
just a series of commands.

00:03:49.644 --> 00:03:54.144
and then it sends the process information back to be stored in memory again.

00:03:54.144 --> 00:03:58.144
This continues until the processed information is ready to be output.

00:03:59.629 --> 00:04:03.309
[Music]

00:04:03.309 --> 00:04:07.309
How a computer outputs information depends on what the computer is designed to do.

00:04:07.309 --> 00:04:09.589
A computer display can show text, photos, and videos.

00:04:09.614 --> 00:04:13.614
videos, or interactive games, even virtual reality.

00:04:13.614 --> 00:04:17.614
The output of a computer may even include signals to control a robot.

00:04:17.614 --> 00:04:19.598
And when computers connect over the internet,

00:04:19.598 --> 00:04:23.598
The output from one computer becomes the input to another and vice versa.

00:04:25.598 --> 00:04:29.583
The computers we use today look really different from the earliest thinking machines.

00:04:29.583 --> 00:04:32.843
And who knows what the computers of tomorrow will be like.

00:04:32.843 --> 00:04:36.623
My hope is that you get to help decide what you want the computers of tomorrow to look

00:04:36.623 --> 00:04:37.623
like.

00:04:37.623 --> 00:04:38.623
But across all computers, regardless of the size of your computer, you can't decide

00:04:38.623 --> 00:04:39.567
what you want the computers of tomorrow to look like.

00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:44.847
of the different types of technology they use, they're always doing the same four things.

00:04:44.847 --> 00:04:49.552
They take in information, they store it as data, they process it, and then they're done.

00:04:49.552 --> 00:04:52.672
they output their results.
